<article class="env-lemma" id="0CER">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CER">Lemma <span data-tag="0CER">15.32.6</span>.</a> Let $A' \to B'$ be a ring map. Let $I \subset A'$ be an ideal. Set $A = A'/I$ and $B = B'/IB'$. Let $f'_1, \ldots , f'_ r \in B'$. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$A' \to B'$ is flat and of finite presentation (for example smooth), 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$I$ is locally nilpotent, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the images $f_1, \ldots , f_ r \in B$ form a quasi-regular sequence, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$B/(f_1, \ldots , f_ r)$ is smooth over $A$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $B'/(f'_1, \ldots , f'_ r)$ is smooth over $A'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Set $C' = B'/(f'_1, \ldots , f'_ r)$ and $C = B/(f_1, \ldots , f_ r)$. Then $A' \to C'$ is of finite presentation. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0CEQ" data-tag="0CEQ">15.32.5</a> we see that $A' \to C'$ is flat. The fibre rings of $A' \to C'$ are equal to the fibre rings of $A \to C$ and hence smooth by assumption (4). It follows that $A' \to C'$ is smooth by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00TF" data-tag="00TF">10.137.16</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>